        "sourceText": "# Concave Hull\n\nTime limit: 1 second. Memory limit: 1024 megabytes. Standard input and output.\n\nA simple polygon is a closed curve in the Euclidean plane formed by straight segments joined end to end. Exactly two segments meet at each endpoint, and the segments have no other intersections.\n\nA simple polygon is convex if, for any two points inside it, the entire segment between them lies in its interior or on its boundary. A simple polygon that is not convex is concave. The figure shows a convex polygon on the left and a concave polygon on the right.\n\n![Convex and concave polygon examples.](105459-B-polygons.png)\n\nGiven $n$ distinct points with no three collinear, select some of them, possibly all, and connect the selected points in any order to form a concave polygon with strictly positive area. Determine the maximum possible area.\n\n## Input Format\n\nThe first line contains $T$ ($1\\le T\\le10^4$), the number of test cases. For each test case, the first line contains $n$ ($3\\le n\\le10^5$), the number of points. The next $n$ lines each contain integers $x_i,y_i$ ($-10^9\\le x_i,y_i\\le10^9$), the coordinates of a point.\n\nAll points are distinct and no three are collinear. The sum of $n$ over all test cases does not exceed $2\\cdot10^5$.\n\n## Output Format\n\nFor each test case, output $-1$ if no concave polygon of strictly positive area can be formed. Otherwise, output a positive integer equal to twice the maximum possible area. This value is always a positive integer.\n\n\n## Sample 1\n\n### Input\n\n```text\n2\n6\n-2 0\n1 -2\n5 2\n0 4\n1 2\n3 1\n4\n0 0\n1 0\n0 1\n1 1\n```\n\n### Output\n\n```text\n40\n-1\n```\n",
